body:
I think the gmail case you reference is better implemented as the "latter". Specifically, the email entry is the overscroll container. In its overscroll to the left is your "left action" item, or a blank piece of content that pushes the email content out of the way to reveal the left action. Similarly, in the overscroll to the right is your "right action" item.

I would imagine these are implemented as two different items in the email's overscroll. They are conceptually two different overscroll areas though. This set up would necessitate two buttons, avoiding ambiguity.

<img width="1307" height="807" alt="Image" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/36fe4435-fe54-4ef8-bc7a-02b2b366a276" />

```html
<style>
#toggle_unread {
  background: lightblue;
  right: 100%;
}
#archive {
  background: lightred;
  left: 100%;
}
</style>

<div overscrollcontainer>
  <span>from: vmpstr to: bramus</span>
  <div id="toggle_unread">
     <button id="toggle_unread_btn" command="toggle-overscroll" commandfor="toggle_unread">toggle unread action</button>
   </div>
  <div id="archive">
     <button id="archive_btn" command="toggle-overscroll" commandfor="archive">archive</button>
   </div>
</div>

<script>
// If we want the click to actually do the action, then do the following. Otherwise, we could use
// overscroll* events and disable the buttons.
toggle_unread_btn.addEventListener("click", e => { ... e.preventDefault(); });
archive_btn.addEventListener("click", e => { ... e.preventDefault(); });
</script>
```
_Disclaimer: I haven't tested this in any prototype, but conceptually that should work_
